-- title:
--   Endomorphism vanishing on the integral lattice is zero
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a natural number and $k$ an integer, and consider the space $S_k(\Gamma_0(N))$ of cusp forms of weight $k$ for the congruence subgroup $\Gamma_0(N)$. Write $L =$ [`CuspForm.intLattice N k`](def/CuspForm_IntegralStructure.html#L3) for the $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of this space generated by those cusp forms $f$ all of whose $q$-expansion coefficients are rational integers, i.e. such that for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ the coefficient `qCoeff f n`, the $n$-th coefficient of the $q$-expansion of $f$ taken with width $1$, lies in the image of $\mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{C}$. The hypothesis [`CuspForm.HasIntegralStructure N k`](def/CuspForm_IntegralStructure.html#L6) asserts that the $\mathbb{C}$-span of $L$, viewed as a subset of $S_k(\Gamma_0(N))$, is the whole space. Let $t$ be a $\mathbb{C}$-linear endomorphism of $S_k(\Gamma_0(N))$, and suppose that $t f = 0$ for every $f \in L$. The conclusion is that $t$ is the zero endomorphism.
--
--   This is the faithfulness statement attached to the integral structure on cusp forms: under the hypothesis that the forms with integral $q$-expansions span the space over $\mathbb{C}$, an endomorphism is determined by its restriction to that lattice. It is used in the finiteness of the Hecke algebra over $\mathbb{Z}$ and in the construction of Hecke eigenforms, and is cited by [`CuspForm.HasIntegralStructure.exists_ne_zero_forall_apply_eq_smul`](thm.html#CuspForm.HasIntegralStructure.exists_ne_zero_forall_apply_eq_smul) and [`CuspForm.HasIntegralStructure.moduleFinite_heckeAlgebra`](thm.html#CuspForm.HasIntegralStructure.moduleFinite_heckeAlgebra), among others.
